{"actor_ref":"claude","class":"draft_history","document_uuid":"01a1a54a-3256-70e3-906b-e0d0d86f21e1","id":"01a1a646-7273-724e-9a5c-5bf1421718a4","idempotency_key":"35a1d3ea5dfb09dcb8dff71cd952f002582034b46c232feb9cf6278c8fbdf20f","occurred_at":"2026-11-04T11:16:37Z","payload":{"note":"The MCP surface is complete: tools, resources and prompts. Tools were already there - 19 tools with the same schemas the in-app assistant uses, asserted to declare an input schema each and NOT to leak internal effect metadata onto the wire. Resources are new: a Resources trait in rbc-ai keeps the protocol testable without a database, and the app implements it over rbc-db for rbc://workspaces, rbc://workspace/{id} (with its boards), rbc://board/{id}, rbc://board/{id}/history, rbc://study/{id}, rbc://study/{id}/chapter/{chapter_id} and rbc://match/{id}/pgn. Every read is a snapshot and the revision travels in the payload, so one picture of a board is distinguishable from the next; MCP marks resources as non-subscribing (R2 owns subscriptions). Prompts are two fixed templates, analyze_position and review_game, asserted to produce identical output for identical arguments - a prompt that varied with server state would not be a prompt. A conformance test asserts MCP exposes no chat, Lua, session, settings or provider-key tool; it matches whole words because a substring check flagged 'evaluate' as containing 'lua', which is exactly the sort of false positive that makes a guard worthless if you let it pass by. Verified live over POST /mcp: resources/list answers, a freshly created board reads back at revision 1, and a prompt renders.","target":"t-6498"},"type":"work.note_added","v":2}
